{:edb/db-before-t 1470 :edb/db-after-t 1471 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534783 50 #inst "2026-10-08T06:46:25.280Z" 13194139534783 true] [17592186151916 1100 "Consider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $1,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,0)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/5d791b30556f41973c8f1a9b8702744afae72458d0ce285de2b7ef8fa19d720a.png)" 13194139534783 true] [17592186151916 1100 "Consider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $1,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,0)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/5d791b30556f41973c8f1a9b8702744afae72458d0ce285de2b7ef8fa19d720a.png)" 13194139534783 false] [17592186151918 1100 "Consider the ellipse centered at $(1,-2)$ with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,-2)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/4724f0bcc1499be95dc8a28584511ecdb86d078b8205ad81a6e836ba1bdd261c.png)" 13194139534783 true] [17592186151918 1100 "Consider the ellipse centered at $(1,-2)$ with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,-2)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/4724f0bcc1499be95dc8a28584511ecdb86d078b8205ad81a6e836ba1bdd261c.png)" 13194139534783 false] [17592186151919 1100 "The semi-ellipse below is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 4\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/6ca367eb7dc7cbd8205cbd85ad6d2503f957defd3f9a10e7b510759f41e0c778.png)" 13194139534783 true] [17592186151919 1100 "The semi-ellipse below is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 4\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/6ca367eb7dc7cbd8205cbd85ad6d2503f957defd3f9a10e7b510759f41e0c778.png)" 13194139534783 false] [17592186151919 1145 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/a64898381b2cf50c71a79a3dbcf02b9a83f320b8c2d1ba542b3beecc05e17625.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0, \\pi\\right],$ we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in\\left[0, \\pi\\right].\n$$" 13194139534783 true] [17592186151919 1145 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/a64898381b2cf50c71a79a3dbcf02b9a83f320b8c2d1ba542b3beecc05e17625.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0, \\pi\\right],$ we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in\\left[0, \\pi\\right].\n$$" 13194139534783 false] [17592186152112 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309906 13194139534783 true] [17592186152112 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309913 13194139534783 true] [17592186152112 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309948 13194139534783 true] [17592186152112 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309989 13194139534783 true] [17592186152113 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309941 13194139534783 true] [17592186152113 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309955 13194139534783 true] [17592186152113 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309967 13194139534783 true] [17592186152114 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309920 13194139534783 true] [17592186152114 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309962 13194139534783 true] [17592186152114 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309996 13194139534783 true] [17592186152114 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310003 13194139534783 true] [17592186152114 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310010 13194139534783 true] [17592186152115 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309927 13194139534783 true] [17592186152115 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309934 13194139534783 true] [17592186152115 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309970 13194139534783 true] [17592186152115 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309975 13194139534783 true] [17592186152115 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309982 13194139534783 true] [17592186152357 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534783 true] [17592186152357 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/0c46972a3968d7110cb08d4b16bfff9267e49b102cf005cdbb21a3630d15c49d.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $7$ and vertical radius $2,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,0)?$" 13194139534783 true] [17592186152357 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2746/Source/Images/q-15856.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $7$ and vertical radius $2$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta  \\in  [0, 2\\pi ),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7, 0)$?" 13194139534783 false] [17592186152357 1145 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=7$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139534783 true] [17592186152725 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534783 true] [17592186152725 1100 "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" 13194139534783 true] [17592186152725 1100 "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$x = 4\\cos \\theta  - 1, y = \\sin \\theta  + 1, 0 \\le \\theta  < 2\\pi $." 13194139534783 false] [17592186152725 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1 \\\\ y-1 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+(y-1)^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+(y-1)^{2}=1.\n$$" 13194139534783 true] [17592186309906 1017 #edb/ref 17592186309907 13194139534783 true] [17592186309906 1089 "q-106391" 13194139534783 true] [17592186309906 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534783 true] [17592186309906 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/6b8b0f54b4bc1e9c7362bd0b22f3dbc2550594855a1225350d366b9a86b0e3f0.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" 13194139534783 true] [17592186309906 1113 #uuid "23823644-896c-5b5c-b67a-e94d42442783" 13194139534783 true] [17592186309906 1145 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=6.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139534783 true] [17592186309907 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309908 13194139534783 true] [17592186309907 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309909 13194139534783 true] [17592186309907 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309910 13194139534783 true] [17592186309907 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309911 13194139534783 true] [17592186309907 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309912 13194139534783 true] [17592186309907 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534783 true] [17592186309907 1041 "selection" 13194139534783 true] [17592186309907 1104 #edb/ref 17592186309910 13194139534783 true] [17592186309907 1110 #uuid "5aec9d10-8416-5a5e-9afb-71967575dc3d" 13194139534783 true] [17592186309908 1034 "x=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=36\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " 13194139534783 true] [17592186309908 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534783 true] [17592186309908 1111 #uuid "9cc5706a-2e0c-519a-ac7e-5ad59af49da6" 13194139534783 true] [17592186309909 1034 "x=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " 13194139534783 true] [17592186309909 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534783 true] [17592186309909 1111 #uuid "f3309619-2f0d-508c-9c77-e83145908dff" 13194139534783 true] [17592186309910 1034 "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " 13194139534783 true] [17592186309910 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534783 true] [17592186309910 1111 #uuid "a79ae17d-f80c-582d-a01c-748fc47d8b54" 13194139534783 true] [17592186309911 1034 "x=36\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " 13194139534783 true] [17592186309911 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534783 true] [17592186309911 1111 #uui