2186309962 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/assets/fcb18bca21a0469f03093edc82cbdec7fbfd410acd434c88a4086876f02d35c6.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(-2,1)$ with horizontal radius $4$ and vertical radius $5,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,1).$\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" 13194139534783 true] [17592186309962 1113 #uuid "54de4c4b-7f01-550f-8d91-6adff7485960" 13194139534783 true] [17592186309962 1145 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-2,1),$ $a=4$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139534783 true] [17592186309963 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309964 13194139534783 true] [17592186309963 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534783 true] [17592186309963 1041 "field-1" 13194139534783 true] [17592186309963 1104 #edb/ref 17592186309964 13194139534783 true] [17592186309963 1110 #uuid "de21acfe-b307-5a0a-8cc5-61d255c2208c" 13194139534783 true] [17592186309964 1034 "4\\cos(\\theta)-2" 13194139534783 true] [17592186309964 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534783 true] [17592186309964 1111 #uuid "a3232c46-d3f1-5e7f-86d1-2790991f90e4" 13194139534783 true] [17592186309965 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309966 13194139534783 true] [17592186309965 1040 #edb/ref 175921